<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zuiderzee</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zuiderzee was a shallow inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> in the north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, enlarged by storm floods in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampen">Kampen</a>, Harderwijk, Enkhuizen and Hoorn traded across it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsluitdijk">Afsluitdijk</a> closed it off in 1932, turning it into the freshwater IJsselmeer, and large parts were then drained to create new polders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zuiderzee.jpg" alt="Zuiderzee" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Janssonius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seven_United_Netherlands_Janssonius_1658.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Zuiderzee was a shallow bay of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> in the northwest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. It extended about 100 km inland and at most 50 km in width, with a depth of about 4 to 5 m, a coastline of about 300 km and an area of 5,000 square kilometres. Its name is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> for southern sea, which shows that it originates in Friesland, to the north. Five small islands lay within it, Wieringen, Urk, Schokland, Pampus and Marken, whose people lived mainly by fishing. Around it fishing villages grew up and several became walled towns with extensive trade, in particular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampen">Kampen</a>, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, Hoorn and Enkhuizen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In classical times the water here was called Lake Flevo by Roman authors, the largest of a region of lowland and freshwater lakes, separated from the sea by a belt of marsh and fen and fed by streams including the Vecht, Eem and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijssel">IJssel</a>. It drained to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlie">Vlie</a>. The Roman geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomponius-mela">Pomponius Mela</a> describes it in 44 CE. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was called Almere, a name in a life of saints by the Anglo-Saxon Boniface in 753, perhaps meaning eel lake.</p><p>Rising sea levels, peat digging by the Frisians and floods turned the lake into an inland sea. The All Saints' Flood of 1170 broke through the barrier dunes near Texel and washed away land between Stavoren, Texel and Medemblik, and its disaster marked the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, since traders from the Hanseatic cities could now reach the area. Further surges in 1173 and 1196 and in the 13th century completed the change, and about 1400 the Zuiderzee had reached its full extent. Storms pushing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> water into the inlet often caused floods and lost ships. On 18 November 1421 a seawall broke and flooded 72 villages and killed about 10,000 people, in the Second St Elizabeth's Flood.</p><p>Closing the Zuiderzee was first proposed in 1667, though a feasible method did not appear until the 20th century. The Flood of January 1916 hastened plans originating from Cornelius Lely, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsluitdijk">Afsluitdijk</a> was built between 1927 and 1932.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>With the completion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsluitdijk">Afsluitdijk</a> in 1932 the Zuiderzee became the freshwater IJsselmeer, and the outer part became part of the Wadden Sea. Large areas were reclaimed by polders: the Wieringermeer Polder in 1930, the Northeast Polder in 1942, Eastern Flevoland in 1957 and Southern Flevoland in 1968. Together they form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuiderzee-works">Zuiderzee Works</a>, and the reclaimed land became the province of Flevoland. The Markerwaard, begun in 1963, was abandoned in the mid-1980s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zuiderzee” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Staveren, also called the Battle of Warns, the Frisians repelled the Hollanders of Count William IV from the eastern coast of the <b>Zuiderzee</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-26">26 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Zutphen was a river-trade centre on the route between the Rhine and the <b>Zuiderzee</b>, and the county included rich farmland in the Achterhoek.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-zutphen">County of Zutphen</a></li></ul></section>
